SOP: Grant Executable Permissions to Script on App Server 2

Objective:
To grant executable permissions to the /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh script on App Server 2 so that all users can execute it.

------------------------------------------------------------

1. Connect to App Server 2
Command:
ssh steve@172.16.238.11

Explanation:
- ssh is the Secure Shell command used to connect to remote Linux servers.
- steve is the username assigned for App Server 2.
- 172.16.238.11 is the IP address of App Server 2.
Once prompted, enter the password for the steve user.

------------------------------------------------------------

2. Switch to Root User
Command:
sudo su -

Explanation:
- sudo temporarily gives administrative (root) privileges.
- su - switches to the root user’s environment.
This allows you to make system-level changes, like modifying file permissions.

------------------------------------------------------------

3. Verify Current Permissions of the Script
Command:
ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Output Example:
---------- 1 root root 40 Nov 13 07:22 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Explanation:
- The output shows no permissions (----------) for anyone.
- The file belongs to root (owner) and group root.
- This means the script cannot currently be executed, read, or modified by any user.

------------------------------------------------------------

4. Grant Execute Permission to All Users
Command:
chmod a+x /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Explanation:
- chmod stands for change mode — used to modify file permissions.
- a+x means add execute permission for all users:
  a → all (owner, group, others)
  +x → add execute permission

Verification:
ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Output Example:
---x--x--x 1 root root 40 Nov 13 07:22 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Meaning:
- Now everyone can execute the file, but no one (even root) can read or modify it.

------------------------------------------------------------

5. Set Recommended Permissions (Read + Execute for All)
Command:
chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Explanation:
- 7 → owner has read (r), write (w), and execute (x)
- 5 → group has read (r) and execute (x)
- 5 → others have read (r) and execute (x)

This ensures:
- Root can view, modify, and run the script.
- All other users can view and execute it, but not modify.

Verification:
ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Output Example:
-rwxr-xr-x 1 root root 40 Nov 13 07:22 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

Meaning:
- Owner (root): can read, write, execute
- Group: can read, execute
- Others: can read, execute

------------------------------------------------------------

6. Validation
To confirm the script is executable:
/tmp/xfusioncorp.sh

If it executes without a “Permission denied” error, permissions are set correctly.

------------------------------------------------------------

7. Summary

| Step | Action | Command | Result |
|------|---------|----------|--------|
| 1 | SSH into App Server 2 | ssh steve@172.16.238.11 | Connected to server |
| 2 | Switch to root user | sudo su - | Gained admin rights |
| 3 | Check current permissions | ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh | No permissions |
| 4 | Add execute permission | chmod a+x /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh | Everyone can execute |
| 5 | Set standard permission | chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh | Owner full, others read/execute |
| 6 | Verify | ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh | -rwxr-xr-x confirmed |

------------------------------------------------------------

Result:
The script /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh is now executable by all users on App Server 2 with correct permissions.
